<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Faroe Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Faroes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Faroe Islands are an archipelago in the North Atlantic between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> and Iceland, settled by Norse farmers in the 9th century. Their assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinganes">Tinganes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torshavn">Tórshavn</a> is one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The islands came under the Norwegian crown around 1035, passed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> into union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and remained Danish after 1814, gaining home rule in 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/faroe-islands.jpg" alt="Faroe Islands" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: It was made by IceKarma (Jeffrey Connell), who released it to the public domain. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Faroe_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Denmark and Norway thus remained under one king of the house of Oldenburg, together with Iceland, the <b>Faroe Islands</b> and the Norse claims to Greenland, while the king also ruled the duchies of Schleswig and Holstein.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The union of crowns with Norway from 1380 to 1814 made Danish the written language of Norway, and Iceland, the <b>Faroe Islands</b> and Greenland stayed under the Danish crown after Norway’s departure, so that Danish administration and language shaped the whole North Atlantic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Norwegians settled the <b>Faroe Islands</b>, Iceland, Shetland, Orkney, the Hebrides and Man, raided and settled in Ireland and northern Britain, and from Iceland reached Greenland and, around 1000, Newfoundland, where the Norse site at L'Anse aux Meadows has been excavated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…area: to England and Ireland, where they founded kingdoms and towns such as York and Dublin; to Normandy; to the <b>Faroe Islands</b>, Iceland, settled from around 870, and Greenland; along the rivers of Russia and Ukraine to Constantinople and the Caspian Sea; and briefly to Newfoundland around 1000.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse speakers also settled in Normandy, whose name means “land of the Northmen”, in the <b>Faroe Islands</b>, in Iceland, and in Greenland, where Erik the Red founded settlements around 985; around 1000 Norse sailors reached North America, at L’Anse aux Meadows in Newfoundland.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-norse">Old Norse · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In the North Atlantic, Norse farmers settled the <b>Faroes</b>, Iceland from about 870 and Greenland from about 985 under Erik the Red, and briefly reached Newfoundland, where the site of L’Anse aux Meadows, found in 1960 and with wood cut there in 1021, confirms the voyages to Vinland…</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
